---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { SITE } from "@/lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={`About ${SITE.name} — what we do and who we write for.`}>
  <section class="container-article py-12 md:py-16">
    <h1 class="font-heading font-extrabold text-4xl md:text-5xl text-slate-deep tracking-tight">
      About this site.
    </h1>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none mt-6">
      <p>
        LLCforLandlords is an LLC-formation reference site for landlords and rental-property
        investors. We write specifically for people who own (or are about to own) investment
        property — not for "any small business owner."
      </p>

      <p>
        That distinction matters. Generic LLC content treats LLC formation as a one-size-fits-all
        decision. Landlord LLC structuring isn't generic: a single-member LLC for a Florida duplex
        has different asset-protection, tax, and financing implications than a multi-member series
        LLC holding ten Texas properties. We cover the differences specifically.
      </p>

      <h2>What we cover</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>LLC formation guidance for rental property — when it makes sense, when it doesn't, and how to actually do it.</li>
        <li>Honest reviews and comparisons of LLC formation services (LegalZoom, ZenBusiness, Northwest Registered Agent, Rocket Lawyer, IncFile).</li>
        <li>Operating agreement templates for single-member and multi-member rental LLCs — reviewed by a licensed attorney before publication.</li>
        <li>State-specific structuring guidance for the most common landlord-favoured states (Wyoming, Florida, Texas).</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What we don't do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>We don't write generic content for non-rental small businesses.</li>
        <li>We don't push the highest-paying affiliate offer over the genuinely-better one.</li>
        <li>We don't replace a CPA or attorney — we make sure you've got specific questions to ask one.</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Read our <a href="/editorial-standards/">editorial standards</a> for the specifics on how we
        source, review, and update content.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
